data.content:
Here's the output:

```json
{
  "Name": "Alice",
  "Age": 30
}
```

**Key point:** `JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase` was added in .NET 9, so on .NET 11 it's available out of the box. Since C# record positional parameters are conventionally lowercase (`name`, `age`), but the generated properties are already PascalCase internally, this policy ensures the serialized JSON keys are `Name` and `Age` — matching PascalCase regardless of how the parameters are declared.

The full program is at `PersonApp/Program.cs`:

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;

var person = new Person("Alice", 30);

var options = new JsonSerializerOptions
{
    PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase,
    WriteIndented = true
};

string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(person, options);
Console.WriteLine(json);

public record Person(string name, int age);
```
